=== general rules ===

# Laravel Development Handbook

## Introduction

When writing code, it is important to keep in mind some general principles and standards that can help improve its quality and readability.

## Naming Conventions

### Class naming

- **Action** — action name with "Action" suffix. Examples: `CreateUserAction`, `DeleteProductAction`, `UpdateCategoryAction`
- **Command** — command name with "Command" suffix. Examples: `GenerateReportCommand`, `ImportDataCommand`, `ExportDataCommand`
- **Controller** — singular with "Controller" suffix. Examples: `UserController`, `ProductController`, `CategoryController`
- **Data** — singular with "Data" suffix. Examples: `UserData`, `ProductData`, `CategoryData`
- **Event** — event name with "Event" suffix. Examples: `UserCreatedEvent`, `OrderCreatedEvent`, `OrderShippedEvent`
- **Exception** — singular with "Exception" suffix. Examples: `ValidationException`, `NotFoundException`, `DuplicateEntryException`
- **Interface** — adjective/noun without suffix. Examples: `Loggable`, `Configurable`, `Exportable`
- **Job** — job name with "Job" suffix. Examples: `SendEmailJob`, `ProcessPaymentJob`, `GenerateReportJob`
- **Mail** — mail name without suffix. Examples: `InvoicePaid`, `OrderShipped`, `PasswordReset`
- **Middleware** — middleware name without suffix. Examples: `Authentication`, `RateLimit`, `Cors`
- **Model** — singular without suffix. Examples: `User`, `Product`, `Category`
- **Notification** — notification name without suffix. Examples: `InvoicePaid`, `OrderShipped`, `PasswordReset`
- **Observer** — singular model name with "Observer" suffix. Examples: `UserObserver`, `ProductObserver`, `CategoryObserver`
- **Policy** — singular model name with "Policy" suffix. Examples: `UserPolicy`, `ProductPolicy`, `CategoryPolicy`
- **Provider** — provider name with "Provider" suffix. Examples: `PaymentProvider`, `StorageProvider`, `EmailProvider`
- **Request** — method name with singular model name and "Request" suffix. Examples: `StoreUserRequest`, `UpdateProductRequest`, `DestroyCategoryRequest`
- **Rule** — rule name without suffix. Examples: `ValidPhoneNumber`, `ValidBankAccount`, `Uppercase`
- **Scope** — scope name with "Scope" suffix. Examples: `ActiveScope`, `NewScope`, `TrendingScope`
- **Support** — support name without suffix. Examples: `OpeningHours`, `Cart`, `Table`
- **Trait** — adjective or prefix "with", without suffix. Examples: `Sortable`, `Searchable`, `Filterable`, `WithForm`, `WithSorting`, `WithFileUploads`

### Code naming

- **Method** — camelCase. Examples: `store`, `massDestroy`, `run`
- **Model property** — snake_case. Examples: `is_active`, `created_at`
- **Class property** — camelCase. Examples: `$isActive`, `$createdAt`
- **Variable** — camelCase. Examples: `$isActive`, `$createdAt`
- **Route** — lowercase, plural. Examples: `users`, `products`, `categories`
- **Route name** — snake_case with dot notation. Examples: `users.show`, `products.index`, `categories.create`

### Database naming

- **Table** — snake_case, plural. Examples: `users`, `products`, `categories`
- **Pivot table** — snake_case, singular model names alphabetically. Examples: `category_product`, `order_user`
- **Table column** — snake_case, without table prefix. Examples: `title`, `price`, `description`
- **Primary key** — `id`
- **Foreign key** — snake_case singular model name with "_id" suffix. Examples: `user_id`, `product_id`, `category_id`

## Structure

### Class structure

When organizing code in the class, follow this order:

- **Traits**
- **Constants**
- **Static properties**
- **Properties**
- **Static methods**
- **Methods**
- **Abstract methods**

Order each category by visibility: **public**, then **protected**, then **private**.

## Commenting

Well-written comments elevate the readability and understanding of code. Readable and understandable code is important in general; comments serve as additional support and boost quality.

### Attributes

When declaring native Laravel attributes such as `$fillable`, you can omit comments and type hints since these properties are not accessed directly. Custom attributes should have a type hint and comments when the name is not self-explanatory.

### Methods

Provide descriptive comments (e.g. PHPDoc) for every method in the codebase.

### Code

Provide descriptive comments for every part of the code that looks unclear, such as complex statements.

=== architecture rules ===

# Architecture

## Model

Models are classes that represent database tables. They allow you to interact with the corresponding data using object-oriented syntax. Models provide an easy way to query, insert, update, and delete data. Read more: [Laravel Eloquent](https://laravel.com/docs/eloquent)

**Create command:** `php artisan make:model Product`

### Naming

Singular without "Model" suffix (`User`, `Product`, `Category`).

### Best practices

- Follow the defined structure: attributes (`$fillable`, `$casts`…), lifecycle methods (`boot()`, `booted()`), relationships, accessors & mutators, other methods.
- Models should contain only Laravel native things (relations, scopes) and database-related code. Business logic belongs in **Support** or **Action** classes.
- Use mass assignment where possible; code for creating/updating is shorter and cleaner.
- Prefer `$fillable` over `$guarded` (generally safer, more control, easy to see column names in the model).

## Controller

Controllers handle requests and responses; they are intermediaries between the database, views, and business logic. Read more: [Laravel Controllers](https://laravel.com/docs/controllers)

**Create command:** `php artisan make:controller UserController`

### Naming

Singular with "Controller" suffix (`UserController`, `ProductController`, `CategoryController`).

### Types

- **resource** — methods for each CRUD operation plus HTML template methods (`create`, `edit`)
- **api** — CRUD methods without HTML template methods
- **invokable** — single-action controllers that do not match resources

### Best practices

- Keep controller methods thin; they should not contain large business logic. They are mainly responsible for returning a response.
- Use resource controllers for CRUD operations; they follow naming conventions.

### Namespacing

When you have multiple controller types (API, Admin…), group them by structure, e.g. `Admin/UserController.php`, `Api/UserController.php`, `UserController.php`.

## Request

Requests encapsulate input data from an HTTP request and provide a convenient way to validate and process user input. Read more: [Form Request Validation](https://laravel.com/docs/validation#form-request-validation)

**Create command:** `php artisan make:request StoreUserRequest`

### Naming

Method name with singular model name and "Request" suffix (`StoreUserRequest`, `UpdateProductRequest`, `UpdateCategoryRequest`).

## Configuration

Configuration is stored in the `config` directory.

- Never access `env()` directly in the application; use it only in configuration files and access values via `config()`.
- Access config values only via `config()`.
- Store custom application settings in a separate config file (e.g. `project.php`).
- Store API keys for third-party services in `services.php`.

## Action

Actions are classes responsible for a single task. Code stays cleaner and simpler.

**Create command:** `php artisan make:action VerifyUserAction` (requires package such as `rockero-cz/laravel-starter-kit`)

### Naming

Action purpose with "Action" suffix (`VerifyUserAction`, `CreateProductAction`, `ReorderCategoryAction`).

### Best practices

- Each action should contain only one public method (e.g. `run()` or `execute()`).
- Action helper methods should be private or protected; multiple helpers may become **Support** classes.

## Support

Support classes group related functions and logic. They are used for functionality shared across multiple parts of the application and are a simpler alternative than services.

**Create command:** `php artisan make:class Support/Cart` (if available in your setup)

### Naming

Support purpose name without "Support" suffix (`Cart`, `OpeningHours`, `Table`).

## Routing

### Route types

- **web** — web-based HTTP requests and responses
- **api** — API requests and responses
- **channels** — real-time broadcasting via websockets
- **console** — custom Artisan CLI commands

### Best practices

- URLs should be plural.
- Each route should have a name.
- Group routes by entities (e.g. by middleware and name prefix).

## Middleware

Middleware filters and modifies incoming HTTP requests (authentication, authorization, session management). Create middleware when you need specific logic for a group of routes. Read more: [Laravel Middleware](https://laravel.com/docs/middleware)

**Create command:** `php artisan make:middleware HandleLocale`

## Observer

Observers listen for model events such as `created`, `updated`, or `deleted`. They keep model classes focused and avoid cluttering them with extra logic. Read more: [Eloquent Observers](https://laravel.com/docs/eloquent#observers)

**Note:** Eloquent mass update queries do not fire model events, so observers are not triggered.

**Create command:** `php artisan make:observer UserObserver --model=User`

### Naming

Singular model name with "Observer" suffix (`UserObserver`, `ProductObserver`, `CategoryObserver`).

## Event

Events trigger and handle actions during application execution; they work with listeners. When an event is dispatched, Laravel notifies all registered listeners. Read more: [Laravel Events](https://laravel.com/docs/events)

Actions are often used instead of events for simplicity; events are still useful for notifications or broadcasting.

**Create command:** `php artisan make:event OrderCreated`

### Dispatching events

Use `OrderCreated::dispatch()` or `event(new OrderCreated())`.

### Listeners

**Create command:** `php artisan make:listener SendOrderCreatedNotification --event=OrderCreated`

## Command

Commands automate tasks. Laravel provides built-in commands; you can create custom ones for repetitive actions. Read more: [Writing Commands](https://laravel.com/docs/artisan#writing-commands)

**Create command:** `php artisan make:command FetchUsers`

### Scheduling commands

Use the [Scheduler](https://laravel.com/docs/scheduling) to run commands at specified intervals or times.

=== database rules ===

# Database

## Column naming

- Primary key should always be `id`.
- Foreign keys should be singular with suffix `_id` (`user_id`, `product_id`, `category_id`).
- Avoid [reserved words](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/keywords.html) when choosing names.

### Recommended names for frequently used columns

- **order_column** — sorting (e.g. drag & drop)
- **company_number** — CIN / Company Identification Number
- **vat_number** — VATIN / VAT ID
- **zip_code** — ZIP / Postal Code

### Names by column type

- **boolean** — e.g. `is_enabled`, `has_reviews`
- **timestamp** — e.g. `published_at`, `valid_from`, `scheduled_for`

Some boolean columns can be converted to timestamps (e.g. `is_finished` → `finished_at`) to store more information in one column.

## Column sorting

- **id** — always first
- **Foreign keys**
- **Other columns** — by priority and context
- **Native timestamps** — `created_at`, `updated_at`

## UUIDs

Use UUIDs for publicly accessible resources or sensitive tables (e.g. bank accounts, orders, invoices). See [UUID and ULID keys](https://laravel.com/docs/eloquent#uuid-and-ulid-keys).

## Indexes

Use indexes on columns that are frequently queried or used in joins to improve performance.

## Migrations

Migrations version-control your database schema and allow structured changes and rollbacks. Read more: [Laravel Migrations](https://laravel.com/docs/migrations)

### Best practices

- During development you can modify existing migrations and run `php artisan migrate:fresh`. In production, create new migrations for every change.
- Preserve column order when adding columns; use `after()` and `before()`.
- Add a short note with `comment()` when column names are not clear.
- You can [squash migrations](https://laravel.com/docs/migrations#squashing-migrations) into a single SQL file over time.

## Seeders

Seeders populate the database with initial or test data. Read more: [Laravel Seeding](https://laravel.com/docs/seeding)

- **Create:** `php artisan make:seeder UserSeeder`
- **Run all:** `php artisan db:seed`
- **Run one:** `php artisan db:seed --class=UserSeeder`

Use the `WithoutModelEvents` trait in seeders when you need to disable model events.

### Naming

Singular with "Seeder" suffix (`UserSeeder`, `ProductSeeder`, `CategorySeeder`).

## Factories

Factories generate fake data for testing and are used in seeders and tests. Read more: [Eloquent Factories](https://laravel.com/docs/eloquent-factories)

**Create command:** `php artisan make:factory UserFactory`

### Naming

Singular model name with "Factory" suffix (`UserFactory`, `ProductFactory`, `CategoryFactory`).

### Usage

- Single record: `User::factory()->create()`
- Single instance: `User::factory()->make()`
- Multiple records: `User::factory()->count(10)->create()`
- With custom attributes: `User::factory()->create(['email' => 'info@example.com'])`

### Factory methods

- **Sequence** — vary attributes (e.g. state, order_column). See [Sequences](https://laravel.com/docs/database-testing#sequences).
- **Recycle** — reuse the same related model instance across relationships. See [Recycling](https://laravel.com/docs/eloquent-factories#recycling-an-existing-model-for-relationships).
- **Relationships** — use `has()`, `for()`, `hasAttached()` or magic methods like `hasPosts(3)`, `forUser()`.

### Custom methods

Define custom state methods on the factory (e.g. `github()`) and use them in tests for consistent setup.

### Factory callbacks

Use `configure()` with `afterMaking()` and `afterCreating()` to run logic after making or creating models.

=== best practices ===

# Best practices

- Accept the standards and principles and follow them; code is easier to maintain and more consistent. Research Laravel codebase, popular packages, and well-known repos when in doubt. "Consistency is key" — Spatie.
- Prefer duplicated code over the wrong abstraction; fixing a bad abstraction can be costly.
- Keep test coverage as high as possible (e.g. at least 70–80%). Example: `php artisan test --coverage --min=80`.
- Use static analysis (e.g. [PHPStan](https://phpstan.org)): `./vendor/bin/phpstan`.
- Use Artisan CLI to create classes: `php artisan make:migration`, `php artisan make:controller`, etc.
- Simplify business logic with action classes (one task per action).
- Group classes into subfolders by resource (e.g. `Actions/User/`, `Actions/Order/`).
- Keep controller methods thin; they are responsible for returning a response.
- Models should contain only database-related code; put business logic in Support or Action classes.
- Never update the database directly; always use migrations for schema changes and version control.

=== testing rules ===

# Testing

We use [Pest](https://pestphp.com/) for automated testing for its simplicity, assertions, and documentation.

**Create command:** `php artisan make:test Actions/VerifyUserActionTest`

## Why write tests?

- Code quality — tests force you to think about the code
- Bug catching — find issues before production
- Time saving — reduce manual testing over time
- Confidence — easier to change and add features

## When to write tests?

Each class that contains logic should have a test.

## What should be tested?

- Code logic (actions, support classes, jobs)
- Livewire components / Controllers
- Routes

## What to keep in the repository?

- During development, write or run tests to verify the change.
- **Remove** tests that pass when they only confirmed a one-off fix and do not guard lasting behaviour (no placeholder or example tests in the tree).
- **Keep** tests that must catch regressions (auth, permissions, critical flows).

## How to write tests?

Prefer **Feature** tests; add **Unit** tests on complex projects. Cover multiple scenarios and edge cases. Assert only what is relevant to the tested class. Keep coverage as high as possible (e.g. at least 70%).

## Helpers

Use Pest hooks to prepare/clean data and create custom methods for reuse.

## Pest hooks

- `beforeEach()` — before each test
- `afterEach()` — after each test
- `beforeAll()` — once before all tests in the file
- `afterAll()` — after all tests in the file

## Custom methods

Define helpers in a test suite or in `Pest.php` for global access (e.g. `asAdmin()`, `mockPayments()`).

## Mocking

Use mocking for events, file uploads, or third-party integrations. See [Laravel Mocking](https://laravel.com/docs/mocking). Use `Http::fake()` for HTTP; use `Http::preventStrayRequests()` to avoid hitting real endpoints.

## Fixtures

Place static test files in a `fixtures` directory and reference them in tests (e.g. `base_path('tests/fixtures/payment.xml')`).

## Naming

Use lowercase, descriptive names for test cases (e.g. `it('has a welcome page', ...)`, `test('payment can be processed', ...)`).

=== tools rules ===

# Tools for effective development

## Development environments (IDE)

- **PHPStorm** — professional IDE for PHP; best for medium/large projects. Plugins for Laravel. Paid. [Download](https://www.jetbrains.com/phpstorm/download)
- **Visual Studio Code** — fast editor for small/medium/large projects. Many plugins; free. [Download](https://code.visualstudio.com/download)
- **Sublime Text** — good for large files (dumps, XML, JSON). [Download](https://www.sublimetext.com/download)

## Debug and testing

- **Ray (Spatie)** — debug PHP without breaking flow; organize dumps by color. [Ray](https://spatie.be/products/ray)
- **Tinkerwell** — run and test code on the project, optionally via SSH. [Tinkerwell](https://tinkerwell.app)
- **Postman** — API testing and development. [Postman](https://www.postman.com/downloads)
- **Insomnia** — API testing; simpler UI than Postman. [Insomnia](https://insomnia.rest/pricing)

## Database

- **TablePlus** — manage databases locally and on servers; SSH, raw queries. [TablePlus](https://tableplus.com)
- **DBngin** — install and manage local PostgreSQL, MySQL, Redis (macOS). [DBngin](https://dbngin.com)

## Emails

- **Mailtrap** — email testing (local and server). [Mailtrap](https://mailtrap.io)
- **Helo** — test emails locally. [Helo](https://usehelo.com)

## Local development

- **Laravel Herd** — app alternative to Valet (macOS). [Herd](https://herd.laravel.com)

## Deployment

- **Laravel Forge** — manage servers, projects, queues, databases. [Forge](https://forge.laravel.com)
- **Envoyer** — zero-downtime deployment, scripts, migrations, queues. [Envoyer](https://envoyer.io)

## Git

- **GitHub Desktop** — simple Git and GitHub UI. [GitHub Desktop](https://desktop.github.com)

## Productivity

- **Raycast** — launcher and productivity (macOS). [Raycast](https://www.raycast.com)
- **Tuple** — pair programming and remote collaboration. [Tuple](https://tuple.app)
- **Slack** / **Discord** — team communication and community channels.
- **iTerm** — advanced terminal (macOS). [iTerm](https://iterm2.com)
- **Fig** — terminal autocomplete and suggestions. [Fig](https://fig.io)

## Others

- **Laravel Shift** — upgrade Laravel and run utilities (e.g. PHPUnit to Pest, Bootstrap to Tailwind). [Laravel Shift](https://laravelshift.com)

